  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 320 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nAn upper level shortwave traverses the Great Plains Saturday evening \ninto Sunday, dragging a cold front along with it. The cold front \nsweeps across our region Saturday night, sending lows into the 30s \nin the higher terrain and much of SE NM. Parts of the Davis and \nGuadalupe Mountains may fall to near or just below the freezing \nmark. Temperatures at most other locations are forecast to dip into \nthe 40s. Highs Sunday and Monday afternoons shall largely top out in \nthe 50s, with 60s in our southernmost counties. Models indicate a \nsurface high near the Great Lakes region ushers a reinforcing shot \nof cold air into our area Tuesday. Consequently, our northern \ncounties may struggle to reach 50 degrees Tuesday and Wednesday \nafternoons, while highs in the 60s become confined to the Rio \nGrande valley by Wednesday. Return flow sets in Wednesday night, \nallowing Thanksgiving's temperatures to be a couple of degrees \nwarmer than Wednesday's, although remaining on the chilly side. \nOvernight lows most nights look to range in the 30s to 40s.\n\nAside from low (10-20%) rain chances over the eastern Permian \nBasin Monday night into Tuesday morning, dry conditions look to \npersist through most of the Long Term period. However, some long- \nrange models suggest rain chances increase just after Thanksgiving,\nin association with another storm system encroaching on our \nregion. More details to follow as we head forward!l",
